-For DMOP-$0$, the particle error grows at most linearly with the number of observations, apart from logarithmic factors.
-The joint mixing assumption (A6-J) gives one fewer factor of $1+\log J$ in this case.
-However, DMOP-$0$ can retain population bias even with arbitrarily many particles.
-
-For any fixed $0<\alpha<1$, the particle bound has the same dependence on $N$ and $J$, with a constant that increases as $\alpha$ approaches one.
-The population bias bound decreases at the same time.
-The exact memory factor is $H_N(\alpha)\leq\min\{N,(1-\alpha)^{-1}\}$, so the finite-horizon particle bound remains finite as $\alpha$ approaches one.
-
-For DMOP-$1$, the population discount bias is zero, and the MSE tends to zero as $J$ increases for fixed $N$.
-Its $N^3/J$ upper bound allows substantially greater particle error for long series.
-These bounds explain the potential benefit of discounting, but do not establish the actual error rates or imply that discounting improves every model.
-
-We analyze geometrically discounted scores through an exact weighted average of scores truncated at finite lags.
-Posterior forgetting controls the population discount bias, while particle approximation and covariance bounds control the Monte Carlo error.
-
-The main difficulties are that measurement gradients depend on simulated histories and that resampling creates dependence between particles and across time.
-Under (A6), we expand each measurement gradient according to the age of its simulator contributions.
-Their geometric decay permits us to sum bounds for finite histories using mixing of the physical particle cloud.
-Under (A6-J), the measurement gradient is already a bounded function of the current state--derivative pair, so we apply the particle bounds directly to that joint process.
