-\label{eq:refined-dmop-alpha-direct-particle-error}
-\end{align}
-The constants suppressed by $\lesssim$ depend only on the fixed model
-bounds and are independent of $N,J$, and $\alpha$.
-\end{lem}
-
-\begin{proof}[Proof.]
-Fix a coordinate $i$.  We first derive the historical $L^2$ estimate
-used below, including the prediction, Bayes-weighting, and resampling
-steps.  Use the existing symbol $\eta_t$ also for the canonical lift of
-the time-$t$ prediction law to complete simulator histories.  Its physical
-last-coordinate marginal is the prediction law defined in
-Section~\ref{appendix:feynman}, and
-\begin{equation}
-\rho_t(f)=\Psi_t(\eta_t)(f)
-=\frac{\eta_t(G_tf)}{\eta_t(G_t)}.
-\label{eq:historical-filter-bayes-target}
-\end{equation}
-The prediction genealogies $\xi_t^P$ form the historical particle model
-of \citet{delmoral18}: conditionally on the time-$(t-1)$ prediction
-cloud, multinomial selection with $G_{t-1}$ followed by $M_t$ produces
-the $J$ time-$t$ prediction paths independently.  Their conditions (A1)
-and (A2) follow from Assumptions {\refAssumpBoundedProcess} and
-{\refAssumpBoundedMeasurement}; as they note after their condition (3.5),
-these conditions for the historical model follow from those for its
-physical marginal.
-
-Let $f$ be any bounded functional of a complete simulator history through
-time $t$, put
-\begin{equation}
-\bar f=f-\eta_t(f),
-\qquad
-R=\operatorname{osc}(f),
-\qquad
-F=R+\bar f.
-\label{eq:historical-positive-shift}
-\end{equation}
-Then $0\leq F\leq2R$ and
-$m(\xi_t^P)(f)-\eta_t(f)=m(\xi_t^P)(F)-\eta_t(F)$.
-Theorem~3.1 of \citet{delmoral18}, applied to $F$ and $F^2$,
-and the $q=2$ comparison following their Equation~(3.10), which is also
-the $q=2$ case of their Theorem~3.3, give, for $J\geq ct$,
-\begin{align}
-\left|\E m(\xi_t^P)(F)-\eta_t(F)\right|
-&\leq C\frac tJ\eta_t(F),
-\label{eq:historical-positive-shift-bias}
-\\
-\E m(\xi_t^P)(F^2)
-&\leq\left(1+C\frac tJ\right)\eta_t(F^2),
-\label{eq:historical-positive-shift-diagonal}
-\\
-\E m(\xi_t^P)^{\odot2}(F\otimes F)
-&\leq \eta_t(F)^2+C\frac tJ\eta_t(F^2).
-\label{eq:historical-positive-shift-off-diagonal}
-\end{align}
-The empirical second-moment identity is
-\begin{align}
-\E\{m(\xi_t^P)(F)^2\}
-&=
-\frac1J\E m(\xi_t^P)(F^2)
-+\left(1-\frac1J\right)
-\E m(\xi_t^P)^{\odot2}(F\otimes F).
-\label{eq:historical-empirical-second-moment}
-\end{align}
-Substitute Equations~
-(\ref{eq:historical-positive-shift-bias})--
-(\ref{eq:historical-positive-shift-off-diagonal}) into Equation~
-(\ref{eq:historical-empirical-second-moment}).  Since
-$\eta_t(F)\leq2R$, $\eta_t(F^2)\leq4R^2$, and $t\geq1$, this gives
-\begin{align}
-&\E\left|m(\xi_t^P)(f)-\eta_t(f)\right|^2                   \notag\\
-&\quad=
-\E\left|m(\xi_t^P)(F)-\eta_t(F)\right|^2                  \notag\\
-&\quad\leq
-\frac CJ\eta_t(F^2)
-+C\frac tJ\eta_t(F^2)
-+2\eta_t(F)
-\left|\E m(\xi_t^P)(F)-\eta_t(F)\right|                  \notag\\
-&\quad\leq C\frac tJ R^2.
-\label{eq:chan-historical-prediction-l2}
-\end{align}
-
-It remains to pass from prediction to filtering.  Conditional on
-$\xi_t^P$, the resampling indices $k_1,\ldots,k_J$ are independent with
-probabilities proportional to $G_t(\xi_{t,a}^P)$, and
-\begin{equation}
-m(\xi_t)(f)=\frac1J\sum_{j=1}^Jf(\xi_{t,k_j}^P).
-\end{equation}
-Therefore
-\begin{align}
-&\E\left[
-m(\xi_t)(f)-\Psi_t\{m(\xi_t^P)\}(f)
-\mid\xi_t^P
-\right]=0,                                                  \notag\\
-&\E\left[
-\left|m(\xi_t)(f)-\Psi_t\{m(\xi_t^P)\}(f)\right|^2
-\mid\xi_t^P
-\right]
-\leq\frac{R^2}{J}.
-\label{eq:historical-filter-resampling-l2}
-\end{align}
-The Bayes-ratio difference satisfies the exact identity
-\begin{align}
-&\Psi_t\{m(\xi_t^P)\}(f)-\rho_t(f)                           \notag\\
-&\quad=
-\frac{
-\{m(\xi_t^P)-\eta_t\}
-\left[G_t\{f-\rho_t(f)\}\right]
-}{m(\xi_t^P)(G_t)}.
-\label{eq:historical-filter-bayes-ratio-identity}
-\end{align}
-The denominator is at least $\underbar G$, while
-\begin{equation}
-\operatorname{osc}
-\left[G_t\{f-\rho_t(f)\}\right]
-\leq2\bar G R.
-\end{equation}
-Equation~(\ref{eq:chan-historical-prediction-l2}) applied to the
-numerator in Equation~
-(\ref{eq:historical-filter-bayes-ratio-identity}) consequently gives
-\begin{equation}
-\left\|
-\Psi_t\{m(\xi_t^P)\}(f)-\rho_t(f)
-\right\|_{L^2}
-\leq C\sqrt{\frac tJ}\,R.
-\label{eq:historical-filter-bayes-ratio-l2}
-\end{equation}
-Combining Equations~(\ref{eq:historical-filter-resampling-l2}) and
-(\ref{eq:historical-filter-bayes-ratio-l2}) by Minkowski's inequality
-proves
-\begin{equation}
-\left\|
-m(\xi_t)(f)-\rho_t(f)
-\right\|_{L^2}
-\leq
-C\sqrt{\frac tJ}\,\operatorname{osc}(f),
-\qquad J\geq c t.
-\label{eq:chan-historical-l2-specialization}
-\end{equation}
-
-The important point is that $f$ in
-Equation~(\ref{eq:chan-historical-l2-specialization}) may depend on the
-complete genealogy.  We therefore apply it directly, without replacing
-$h_{k,i}$ by a function of $X_k$.  Equation~
-(\ref{eq:bounded-realized-measurement-score}) gives
-\begin{align}
-\operatorname{osc}
-\left(\sum_{k=1}^{t}\alpha^{t-k}h_{k,i}\right)
-&\leq
-2G'(\theta)\sum_{k=1}^{t}\alpha^{t-k}.
-\label{eq:historical-discounted-score-oscillation}
-\end{align}
-Equations~(\ref{eq:chan-historical-l2-specialization}) and
-(\ref{eq:historical-discounted-score-oscillation}) consequently give,
-for $J\geq cN$ and every $t\leq N$,
-\begin{align}
-&\left\|
-m(\xi_t)\left(\sum_{k=1}^{t}\alpha^{t-k}h_{k,i}\right)
--\rho_t\left(\sum_{k=1}^{t}\alpha^{t-k}h_{k,i}\right)
-\right\|_{L^2}                                               \notag\\
-&\qquad\leq
-CG'(\theta)\sqrt{\frac tJ}
-\sum_{k=1}^{t}\alpha^{t-k}.
-\label{eq:historical-discounted-score-l2}
-\end{align}
-
-Subtract the two full-score identities in Equations~
-(\ref{eq:population-full-score-summation-by-parts}) and
-(\ref{eq:full-score-summation-by-parts}), and take the $i$th coordinate.
-Apply Minkowski's inequality and then Equation~
-(\ref{eq:historical-discounted-score-l2}).  The finite identity
-\begin{equation}
-(1-\alpha)\sum_{k=1}^{t}\alpha^{t-k}=1-\alpha^t
-\end{equation}
-then gives
-\begin{align}
-&\left\|
-\partial_{\theta_i}\hat\ell^\alpha(\theta)
--\partial_{\theta_i}\ell^\alpha(\theta)
-\right\|_{L^2}                                               \notag\\
-&\quad\leq
-\frac{CG'(\theta)}{\sqrt J}
-\left\{
-\sum_{t=1}^{N-1}(1-\alpha^t)\sqrt t
-+\sqrt N\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}\alpha^r
-\right\}.
-\label{eq:historical-full-score-l2}
-\end{align}
-Squaring Equation~(\ref{eq:historical-full-score-l2}) and summing over
-$i=1,\ldots,p$ proves the first member of the minimum in
-Equation~(\ref{eq:refined-dmop-alpha-direct-particle-error}) when
-$J\geq cN$.
-
-We next prove the pathwise member of the minimum.  Equation~
-(\ref{eq:bounded-realized-measurement-score}) and the coefficient identity
-\begin{align}
-&(1-\alpha)\sum_{t=1}^{N-1}\sum_{k=1}^{t}\alpha^{t-k}
-+\sum_{k=1}^{N}\alpha^{N-k}                                  \notag\\
-&\qquad=
-\sum_{t=1}^{N-1}(1-\alpha^t)+\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}\alpha^r
-=N
-\label{eq:generic-full-score-coefficient-identity}
-\end{align}
-give, for every coordinate,
-\begin{equation}
-\left|\partial_{\theta_i}\hat\ell^\alpha(\theta)\right|
-\leq NG'(\theta),
-\qquad
-\left|\partial_{\theta_i}\ell^\alpha(\theta)\right|
-\leq NG'(\theta).
-\end{equation}
-Hence
-\begin{equation}
-\E\left\|
-\nabla_\theta\hat\ell^\alpha(\theta)
--\nabla_\theta\ell^\alpha(\theta)
-\right\|_2^2
-\leq4pN^2G'(\theta)^2.
-\label{eq:generic-full-score-pathwise-cap}
-\end{equation}
-For $J<cN$, Equation~
-(\ref{eq:generic-full-score-pathwise-cap}) supplies the required bound;
-for $J\geq cN$, use Equation~
-(\ref{eq:historical-full-score-l2}).  Since the population target is
-deterministic,
-\begin{equation}
-\Var\left\{\nabla_\theta\hat\ell^\alpha(\theta)\right\}
-\leq
-\E\left\|
-\nabla_\theta\hat\ell^\alpha(\theta)
--\nabla_\theta\ell^\alpha(\theta)
-\right\|_2^2.
-\end{equation}
-
-It remains to simplify the finite sum.  Its upper bound follows from
-\begin{align}
-\sum_{t=1}^{N-1}(1-\alpha^t)\sqrt t
-+\sqrt N\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}\alpha^r
-&\leq
-\sum_{t=1}^{N-1}\sqrt t+N^{3/2}
-\leq2N^{3/2}.
-\end{align}
-For the matching lower order, if
-$\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}\alpha^r\geq N/2$, the terminal term is at least
-$N^{3/2}/2$.  Otherwise
-\begin{equation}
-\sum_{t=1}^{N-1}(1-\alpha^t)
-=N-\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}\alpha^r>N/2.
-\end{equation}
-The sequence $1-\alpha^t$ is nondecreasing, so its terms with
-$t\geq N/2$ contain at least half of this sum.  Therefore the first term
-in braces in Equation~(\ref{eq:historical-full-score-l2}) is at least
-$N^{3/2}/(4\sqrt2)$.  Thus the braces have order $N^{3/2}$ uniformly in
-$\alpha$.  This proves the last line of
-Equation~(\ref{eq:refined-dmop-alpha-direct-particle-error}).
-\end{proof}
-
-\begin{cor}[Particle bias of the DMOP-$\alpha$ score]
-\label{lem:dmop-full-particle-bias}
-Under the conditions of Lemma~\ref{lem:dmop-alpha-variance},
-\begin{equation}
-\left\|
-\E\nabla_\theta\hat\ell^\alpha(\theta)
--\nabla_\theta\ell^\alpha(\theta)
-\right\|_2
-\lesssim
-\sqrt p\,G'(\theta)
-\min\left\{N,\frac{N^2}{J}\right\}.
-\label{eq:full-score-particle-bias-direct}
-\end{equation}
-\end{cor}
-
-\begin{proof}[Proof.]
-We first derive the filtered historical bias used here.  For any bounded
-historical functional $f$, Equation~
-(\ref{eq:historical-filter-bayes-ratio-identity}) can be expanded as
-\begin{align}
-&\Psi_t\{m(\xi_t^P)\}(f)-\rho_t(f)                           \notag\\
-&\quad=
-\frac{\{m(\xi_t^P)-\eta_t\}
-\left[G_t\{f-\rho_t(f)\}\right]}{\eta_t(G_t)}               \notag\\
-&\qquad-
-\frac{
-\{m(\xi_t^P)-\eta_t\}\left[G_t\{f-\rho_t(f)\}\right]
-\{m(\xi_t^P)-\eta_t\}(G_t)
-}{
-\eta_t(G_t)m(\xi_t^P)(G_t)
-}.
-\label{eq:historical-filter-bias-ratio-expansion}
-\end{align}
-The expectation of the first term in Equation~
-(\ref{eq:historical-filter-bias-ratio-expansion}) has absolute value at
-most $Ct\operatorname{osc}(f)/J$ by Equation~
-(\ref{eq:historical-positive-shift-bias}), applied after adding a
-constant to make its numerator function nonnegative.  Both denominators
-are at least $\underbar G$.  By Cauchy--Schwarz and Equation~
-(\ref{eq:chan-historical-prediction-l2}), the expectation of the absolute
-value of the second numerator is at most
-\begin{align}
-&\left\|
-\{m(\xi_t^P)-\eta_t\}
-\left[G_t\{f-\rho_t(f)\}\right]
-\right\|_{L^2}
-\left\|
-\{m(\xi_t^P)-\eta_t\}(G_t)
-\right\|_{L^2}                                               \notag\\
-&\qquad\leq
-C\frac tJ\operatorname{osc}(f).
-\label{eq:historical-filter-bias-product-bound}
-\end{align}
-Finally, Equation~(\ref{eq:historical-filter-resampling-l2}) has
-conditional mean zero.  Taking expectations in the decomposition into
-the resampling and Bayes-ratio terms therefore gives
-\begin{equation}
-\left|\E m(\xi_t)(f)-\rho_t(f)\right|
-\leq C\frac tJ\operatorname{osc}(f),
-\qquad J\geq ct.
-\label{eq:historical-filter-bias-bound}
-\end{equation}
-
-Set $f=\sum_{k=1}^{t}\alpha^{t-k}h_{k,i}$ in Equation~
-(\ref{eq:historical-filter-bias-bound}).  Equation~
-(\ref{eq:historical-discounted-score-oscillation}) gives, for
-$J\geq cN$,
-\begin{align}
-&\left|
-\E m(\xi_t)\left(\sum_{k=1}^{t}\alpha^{t-k}h_{k,i}\right)
--\rho_t\left(\sum_{k=1}^{t}\alpha^{t-k}h_{k,i}\right)
-\right|                                                     \notag\\
-&\qquad\leq
-C\frac tJ G'(\theta)\sum_{k=1}^{t}\alpha^{t-k}.
-\end{align}
-Apply the full-score summation-by-parts identities and the triangle
-inequality exactly as in Equation~
-(\ref{eq:historical-full-score-l2}).  Then
-\begin{align}
-&\left|
-\E\partial_{\theta_i}\hat\ell^\alpha(\theta)
--\partial_{\theta_i}\ell^\alpha(\theta)
-\right|                                                     \notag\\
-&\quad\leq
-\frac{CG'(\theta)}J
-\left\{
-\sum_{t=1}^{N-1}t(1-\alpha^t)
-+N\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}\alpha^r
-\right\}                                                   \notag\\
-&\quad\leq\frac{CN^2G'(\theta)}J.
-\end{align}
-The pathwise bound is $2NG'(\theta)$ in each coordinate by
-Equation~(\ref{eq:generic-full-score-pathwise-cap}).  Combining the two
-ranges of $J$ and taking the Euclidean norm proves
-Equation~(\ref{eq:full-score-particle-bias-direct}).
-\end{proof}
